  {
    "text": "To distinguish X from Y, we require a measure of the extent to which the values of the random variables spread out. In Statistics, we have studied that the variance is a measure of the variability or spread in the values of a random variable may be measured by variance.",
    "page_number": 4,
    "metadata": {
      "source_url": "page_4.png",
      "source_type": "image",
      "topics": [
        "Variance"
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        "Measure of Spread",
        "Variability"
      ],
      "chunk_type": "explanation",
      "difficulty_level": "basic",
      "prerequisites": [
        "Expected Value",
        "Probability Distributions"
      ]
    },
    "chunk_index": 5
  },
  {
    "text": "Definition 7 Let X be a random variable whose possible values $x_1, x_2,...,x_n$ occur with probabilities $p(x_1), p(x_2),..., p(x_n)$ respectively.\nLet $\\mu = E(X)$ be the mean of X. The variance of X, denoted by $\\text{Var}(X)$ or $\\sigma_x^2$ is defined as\n$$ \\sigma_x^2 = \\text{Var}(X) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i - \\mu)^2 p(x_i) $$\nor equivalently\n$$ \\sigma_x^2 = E(X - \\mu)^2 $$",
    "page_number": 4,
    "metadata": {
      "source_url": "page_4.png",
      "source_type": "image",
      "topics": [
        "Variance"
      ],
      "subtopics": [
        "Definition of Variance",
        "Formula for Variance"
      ],
      "chunk_type": "definition",
      "difficulty_level": "basic",
      "prerequisites": [
        "Random Variables",
        "Probability Distributions",
        "Expected Value"
      ]
    },
    "chunk_index": 6
